i'm jane secker and you're listening to  episode six  of storycast 21. in 2017  a chance meeting between sky news  correspondent ashish joshi  and a 12 year old girl in one of the  world's biggest refugee camps  profoundly impacted both their lives  this is war child  [Music]  the exodus began at the end of august  but we're still  seeing thousands of rohingya fleeing  across the border from myanmar  into bangladesh my name is ashish joshi  i'm a sky news correspondent and i was  dispatched  to bangladesh in september 2017 to cover  the rohingya crisis  the rohingya are a stateless ethnic  group who predominantly follow islam  and live in rakhine state myanmar  also known as burma it's the biggest  country in mainland south east asia  with a population of 54 million we're  tying a tourniquet  i'd been to cover the initial stages of  the rohingya crisis  this happened across across india the  exodus the streaming of thousands of  people across the border  myanmar is bordered by bangladesh india  china laos  and thailand and is one of only four  countries in the world  under military rule  [Music]  since it got its independence in 1948  myanmar has been ravaged by conflict  no ethnic group has suffered more than  the rohingya  then in 2017 the military began a  campaign  of ethnic cleansing against the 1.4  million rohingya living there  using murder rape and destruction  on a massive scale this resulted in more  than  seven hundred thousand rohingya fleeing  to neighboring bangladesh  it's hard to grasp the sheer enormity  of what happened here and what is  happening  so we're going to tell you the huge  story of the rohingya  through the eyes of one young girl  a girl i've never forgotten and who i  first met  in 2017 in kutapalong refugee camp  in bangladesh  and i was born in burma in 2006.  rohingya children like zora have seen  things  no child should see she survived the  massacre at tollatoli  a village where hundreds were  slaughtered  azura said she had traveled from a small  village  called tulatoli on the border with  bangladesh  when i was with my parents and siblings  before the military came  my life was very good  [Music]  my home was very beautiful there were  trees surrounding my home  and our village was beautiful too  the houses were separate from each other  and people could go from one home to  another home  my father had lots of land and property  we had a banana tree garden  and my father used to sell bananas my  mother was a housewife  and i had a sister who used to cook food  for us  [Music]  i used to graze the cows and then i used  to play with friends  and sit with my parents we were five  siblings  two sisters and three brothers  the conflicts between the myanmar  authorities in the rohingya has been  going on  for years the  rohingya identify with  their neighbors in in bangladesh they  live within the rakhine region which is  the borderland area  between myanmar and bangladesh  and they're not accepted as bangladeshi  they're not accepted as  legitimate myanmar citizens across the  stream from this border guards gun are  thousands of rohingya refugees  willing to risk their lives to get  across  they're not allowed to vote they have no  rights  they are victimized  and targeted by the police and by  the military they're caught between a  country that wants them  out and one that doesn't want them in  in august 2017 just before this great  exodus  there was a report of an attack on a  myanmar a soldier's outpost  and this resulted in  some deaths and  the reciprocation from the myanmar  authorities  was brutal  i remember it was a wednesday when the  military came  they started shooting the people were  taken to the shore and my mother was  chopped to death  i was lying nearby the riverside after i  saw my mother chopped to death  people started jumping into the river  and i also jumped  hello i got a bullet injury near my hip  in the rear side i was floating on the  water  then i was found lying down near the  riverbank unconscious  i was carried by wycombe people but i  didn't know anything as i was  unconscious  men were shot women were raped  and then killed afterwards bodies were  thrown onto fires  pits were dug and filled with mass  graves  and the killings that took place there  almost nobody  was left alive in tulatori zora  along with her younger brother cairo  survived this mask  after i got the bullet injury i thought  i would die  i could not tolerate the pain and  burning in my injured area  why kung people carried me and gave me  food but i could not eat  i did not know where i was going all the  people carrying me  only alan knows what was happening  my understanding of zora's journey is it  would have been similar to the one  made by thousands of refugees  it would have been up mountains  through jungles across forests  and wading across  a maze crisscrossing waterways rivers  we know that the borderlands in some  places was  heavily mined by  the myanmar army  near the riverbank at the bangladesh  crossing people saw me bleeding  and said that they should help me to  cross the river first  this is a coastal area so there are  large  tidal waterways which separates  myanmar from bangladesh there were so  many people on the boat  and i did not know about bangladesh as i  never visited this country  it was a dangerous stretch of water to  navigate at the best of times  almost every day we would hear more  stories  about boats capsizing  dozens and dozens of people drowning  with every crossing  [Music]  it took three days to reach bangladesh i  was taken to hospital and treated there  and then i came to kuta prolonged camp  [Music]  when i came to the camp i saw so many  shelters and so many people  i met with my brother in the camp  and i was worried about how to manage  our food and worried about my gunshot  injuries  [Music]  refugee camp in bangladesh it's on the  border  with myanmar and it's teeming  with up to a million people now  imagine taking two small children  and having them stand outside  wembley stadium when there must be  20 30 40 50 000 people there  all pushing and brushing past you  imagine taking two  young children too traumatized children  and just putting them down in the middle  of that thoroughfare  and saying you're on your own now  now orphaned at 12 and left alone to  care for her 10 year old brother  i asked zoro what she thought would  happen  i was feeling better than a hospital now  that i was in the camp  i stayed with the people of wycombe who  carried me from myanmar to bangladesh  they protected me and my brother in the  camp you liked it  i felt safe in the early weeks because  nobody could hit me or kill me here  like myanmar after i spent the morning  with zoro i asked  my fixer to use his contacts and i said  i understand traffickers might be  targeting  the camp and he looked at me and he said  the traffickers live in the camps  they're rohingya traffickers  and they target young rohingya girls  and they will kidnap these girls and  sell them into  the bangladeshi sex trade  we're on one of the main roads leading  out of the refugee camp and this  is a police checkpoint they're here to  stop the rohingyas from leaving  but human traffickers are ruthless  people  there is a huge markets in the middle  east  in india and other countries in the  region for  young bangladeshi and nepali girls  i heard that children were being  kidnapped and i was very worried about  how i could escape  if anything like that happened in the  camp because i was also sick with my  gunshot injuries  so when he told me that these  traffickers actually lived and worked  in these refugee camps he said it would  be relatively easy to find one  and that we were to rendezvous in  cox's bizarre the coastal city which is  a few hours drive from  kutaplan camp and  he took me to a parked van  slid open the door of the van  and inside was a  man who must have been somewhere in his  late 20s early 30s  garcim is a human trafficker and belongs  to a gang of pimps  targeting women and young girls inside  could  belong and i asked him who are the girls  you  look for he said we look for young girls  orphans are good because they won't be  there are missed and more rohingya  coming to the camps they've lost  everything they're desperate  so the number of pimps in the refugee  camps has grown and it's big business  i remember him saying he said i've never  had it so good these are easy pickings  this is  the easiest we've ever had it now you  can even find people sleeping rough on  the streets with no shelter  and they have unmarried girls with them  so my business is thriving  there are lines and lines of children  without any protection and we can just  literally walk up to these  children pick them up bundle them into  vans  and drive off and they won't be missed  it was just then it was in a split  second i thought  oh my god he's talking about children  like zoro  zura shows me where she was shot in the  thigh we had to file  for that evening's news bulletin  now orphaned at 12. and then it was  time to leave  i live quite close to heathrow airport  as soon as i land  i become a husband and a father again  and  i was looking at my children as they  walked through the school gates  i just started welling up it was all to  do with the fact  that i had a daughter and a son who  looked like zora  and her brother cairo i had met hours  after meeting her i'd met  a man who'd sat in a van and told me  he was ready to smuggle zora out of the  country  and into a brothel into a red light  district i thought  i can't let that happen i've got to do  something to help  [Music]  so i set up a crowdfunding page i just  wanted to know  that she could put her head down at  night  and not have to worry about where she  would wake up the next morning that was  it that's all i wanted  and i think i was having lunch with a  friend of mine  and then the first alert went and the  second and the third  and then i understood what was happening  my phone the notifications on my phone  it was  it just wouldn't stop and within the  space 24 hours would have 15 000 pounds  we had to pay off her outstanding  medical  bills and then it was a much longer term  approach to enhancing zura's life  [Music]  sky news helped me and mr shafil rahman  also helped me he gave me a sewing  machine  and also money for treatment and to  provide education  zora and her brother are being schooled  in what's now their home  in kutpalang camp i used a fixer in  bangladesh to help me  so she has her own sewing machine she's  taking  classes she's learning how to sew and  that makes her  self-sufficient zora is only 14 or 15  now so she's still only a child but  she's  thinking and acting like a young woman  she's looking ahead  to her life i see my future with sewing  as i have this skill  i'm a tailor i can make dresses i'm also  thinking for my brother  about how i can improve his life zura is  not only looking after herself but she's  looking after her brother  the rest of her family are all dead  her guardians now her new family if you  like  are people who have been moved by her  story  [Music]  one day actually i thought about this  before one day i would actually  like to take my children back to meet  zora  and sit down in a  cafe and eat  and drink and perhaps even laugh  we cannot go out of the house in the  camp that is the only thing  but still better than myanmar and i feel  safer here in the camp  than myanmar that's what i think  despite the hardship they're about to  face these may be the fortunate ones  many rohingya have died trying to make  it this far  this body was found washed up on a beach  this year in february 2021 the military  seized power in myanmar  the situation there remains unstable  meanwhile hundreds of thousands of  rohingya refugees remain displaced war  child  was written and produced by robert  mulhern with thanks to shafoor rahman  who coordinated recordings made in  bangladesh  if you want to learn more about this  story go to sky news dot com  forward slash storycast 21.  next time we're in the uk where the  hopes and dreams of a city  fall on the shoulders of a one-time  supermarket  shelf stacker it was ideal to me  honestly just walk down  five minutes down to work and then get  my shift done and then  in all you ever dream about is playing  at wembley

